---
// SAWTOOTH — the two definitions of a second, and a correction that has only ever gone one way.
//
// It calls itself a WORK and not an instrument, and the distinction is load-bearing rather than
// modest: an instrument here is something that keeps measuring and keeps finding (Admissions
// discovers a new version every week). This draws one long public record carefully and finds
// nothing the record did not already hold. Its own audit says exactly that, and a page whose
// header claimed otherwise would be the first thing the audit contradicted.
//
// Every figure and every number below is derived at build time by src/lib/sawtooth/* from one
// committed file: the IERS EOP 14 C04 series, one row per day since 1962, snapshotted into
// src/data/sawtooth/rotation.json by scripts/sawtooth/fetch.ts. Nothing is fetched at view time
// and no digit is typed into a sentence — where a sentence needs a number it takes it as a value.
//
// The two claims that come from outside the series carry their sources in the text, because they
// are the only things here a reader cannot recompute: the CGPM's decision of 2022 to retire the
// leap second, and Agnew's 2024 measurement that melting ice has postponed the first negative one.
import type { Locale } from '@/lib/site'
import { sawtooth } from '@/lib/sawtooth/series'
import { decades, teeth, together, TOLERANCE, WIDE } from '@/lib/sawtooth/figure'
import SheetHead from '@/components/experiments/SheetHead.astro'
import SheetFoot from '@/components/experiments/SheetFoot.astro'
import '@/styles/experiment-sheet.css'
import '@/styles/sawtooth.css'

interface Props { locale: Locale }
const {} = Astro.props as Props

const s = sawtooth()
const one = together(s)
const two = teeth(s)
const barsBox = { ...WIDE, height: 165, padTop: 22 }
const bars = decades(s, barsBox)

const ms = (n: number) => `${n >= 0 ? '+' : '−'}${Math.abs(n).toFixed(3)}`
const secs = (n: number) => n.toFixed(2)
const firstDecade = s.decades[0]
const lastDecade = s.decades.at(-1)!
const span = `${s.first.slice(0, 4)}–${s.last.slice(0, 4)}`
---

<main id="main" class="sheet mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl flex-1 px-4 py-10 sm:py-14">
  <SheetHead
    kicker="A work, from one long record"
    title="Sawtooth"
    badges={['DERIVED AT BUILD', 'REPRESENTATION & MEMORY']}
  >
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-fg-muted">
      A second used to be a slice of the day: the Earth turns, cut it into eighty-six thousand four
      hundred. In 1967 the second was redefined on caesium, and the two stopped agreeing. The Earth
      is a poor clock — it runs long — so <b class="text-fg">{s.leaps.length}</b> times a leap
      second has been shoved into the civil clock to keep it in step with the sky. Then the Earth
      sped up, and the correction ran out of things to correct.
    </p>
  </SheetHead>

  {/* ── plate one: the size of the intervention ─────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">What the correction holds back</p>
    <p class="mb-5 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Both lines below are on <b class="text-fg">one scale</b>. The falling one is the
      counterfactual: where a clock that followed the turning Earth would stand today if nobody had
      ever intervened — <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{secs(s.drift)} seconds</b> adrift, summed
      one day's excess at a time across {span}. The flat one is the clock the world actually
      keeps. Its flatness is not the Earth behaving. It is
      <b class="text-fg">{s.leaps.length} interventions</b>, each marked.
    </p>

    <figure class="saw-plate">
      <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${WIDE.width} ${WIDE.height}`} role="img" class="saw-svg"
        aria-label={`Two lines on one scale across ${span}. The uncorrected line falls to ${secs(s.drift)} seconds; the clock actually kept stays flat, pulled back ${s.leaps.length} times.`}>
        <g class="saw-grid">
          {one.ticks.map((t) => <line x1={t.x} y1={WIDE.padTop} x2={t.x} y2={WIDE.height - WIDE.padBottom} />)}
          <line class="saw-zero" x1={WIDE.padX} y1={one.zeroY} x2={WIDE.width - WIDE.padX} y2={one.zeroY} />
        </g>
        <g class="saw-leaps">
          {one.leapX.map((x) => <line x1={x} y1={one.zeroY - 7} x2={x} y2={one.zeroY + 7} />)}
        </g>
        <path class="saw-line saw-uncorrected" d={one.paths[0].d} />
        <path class="saw-line saw-observed" d={one.paths[1].d} />
        <g class="saw-tick-labels">
          {one.ticks.map((t) => <text x={t.x} y={WIDE.height - 6} text-anchor="middle">{t.label}</text>)}
        </g>
        <g class="saw-axis">
          {one.axis.map((a) => <text x={WIDE.padX + 3} y={a.textY}>{a.label}</text>)}
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="saw-legend">
        <span class="saw-key saw-key-uncorrected">if nobody had intervened</span>
        <span class="saw-key saw-key-observed">the clock the world keeps</span>
        <span class="saw-key saw-key-leap">a leap second</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  {/* ── plate two: the teeth ────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">The teeth, and where they stop</p>
    <p class="mb-5 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      The same flat line, alone, on its own scale. Now it is a saw: a slide as the Earth falls
      behind, then a snap back of exactly one second. The rule is that the two clocks may never
      stand more than nine tenths of a second apart, and for {span} they never have. Read it to the
      right-hand end. The last tooth was cut on
      <b class="text-fg">{s.lastLeap}</b>, <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{s.yearsSinceLeap}</b> years
      ago, and since then the line drifts the <i>other</i> way — up, away from the edge it used to
      fall toward. There has been nothing to correct.
    </p>

    <figure class="saw-plate">
      <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${WIDE.width} ${WIDE.height}`} role="img" class="saw-svg"
        aria-label={`The published difference between Earth time and atomic time across ${span}: ${s.leaps.length} slides of about a second, each ended by a one-second snap back, and a final stretch after ${s.lastLeap} that drifts the opposite way.`}>
        <g class="saw-grid">
          {two.ticks.map((t) => <line x1={t.x} y1={WIDE.padTop} x2={t.x} y2={WIDE.height - WIDE.padBottom} />)}
          <line class="saw-zero" x1={WIDE.padX} y1={two.zeroY} x2={WIDE.width - WIDE.padX} y2={two.zeroY} />
        </g>
        <g class="saw-leaps">
          {two.leapX.map((x) => <line x1={x} y1={WIDE.padTop} x2={x} y2={WIDE.height - WIDE.padBottom} />)}
        </g>
        <g class="saw-bound">
          {[two.axis[0], two.axis[2]].map((a) => <line x1={WIDE.padX} y1={a.y} x2={WIDE.width - WIDE.padX} y2={a.y} />)}
        </g>
        <path class="saw-line saw-observed" d={two.paths[0].d} />
        <g class="saw-tick-labels">
          {two.ticks.map((t) => <text x={t.x} y={WIDE.height - 6} text-anchor="middle">{t.label}</text>)}
        </g>
        <g class="saw-axis">
          {two.axis.map((a) => <text x={WIDE.padX + 3} y={a.textY}>{a.label}</text>)}
        </g>
      </svg>
    </figure>
  </section>

  {/* ── plate three: the sign change ────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">The day, by the decade</p>
    <p class="mb-5 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      How much longer than eighty-six thousand four hundred seconds an average day ran, decade by
      decade. The bar that matters is the last one: for the first time in the record the mean day
      is <b class="text-fg">short</b>. Of the <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{s.shortDays.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</b>
      days on file that ran under the full count, <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{s.shortDaysSince2020.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</b>
      fall in this decade alone. The shortest day ever measured was
      <b class="text-fg">{s.shortest.date}</b>, at <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{ms(s.shortest.ms)} ms</b>;
      the longest was <b class="text-fg">{s.longest.date}</b>, at <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{ms(s.longest.ms)} ms</b>.
    </p>

    <figure class="saw-plate">
      <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${WIDE.width} 165`} role="img" class="saw-svg"
        aria-label={`Mean excess length of a day by decade, from ${ms(firstDecade.ms)} milliseconds in the ${firstDecade.decade}s to ${ms(lastDecade.ms)} in the ${lastDecade.decade}s — the first decade of the record whose average day runs short.`}>
        <line class="saw-zero" x1={WIDE.padX} y1={bars.zeroY} x2={WIDE.width - WIDE.padX} y2={bars.zeroY} />
        {bars.bars.map((b) => (
          <g class={b.negative ? 'saw-bar saw-bar-short' : 'saw-bar'}>
            <rect x={b.x} y={b.y} width={b.width} height={Math.max(b.height, 0.6)} />
            <text x={b.x + b.width / 2} y={bars.decadeY} text-anchor="middle">{b.decade}s</text>
            <text class="saw-bar-value" x={b.x + b.width / 2} y={b.labelY} text-anchor="middle">{ms(b.ms)}</text>
          </g>
        ))}
      </svg>
    </figure>
  </section>

  {/* ── what it means ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">A second nobody has ever deleted</p>
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <p>
        Every one of the {s.leaps.length} corrections added a second. If the Earth keeps running
        fast, the next one has to take one away — a minute of fifty-nine seconds, a clock that
        skips. No computer has ever been asked to do that. The software that handles the
        additions, badly and famously, has never once been tested against the subtraction.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things have happened to that prospect, and they pull in opposite directions.
        In <b class="text-fg">2024</b> a measurement showed that meltwater from Greenland and
        Antarctica is moving mass toward the equator and slowing the Earth back down — postponing
        the first negative leap second by about three years, to around 2029
        (<a class="sheet-link" rel="noopener" href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07170-0">Agnew, <i>Nature</i> 628, 2024</a>).
        The clock is being held back by the ice giving way. And in
        <b class="text-fg">2022</b> the General Conference on Weights and Measures resolved to stop
        inserting leap seconds altogether by 2035, and to let the two definitions simply drift
        apart — the gap to be capped at some larger figure, to be settled this year
        (<a class="sheet-link" rel="noopener" href="https://www.bipm.org/en/cgpm-2022/resolution-4">CGPM Resolution 4, 2022</a>).
      </p>
      <p class="text-fg">
        So the saw in the second plate is finished either way. Either its next tooth cuts backwards
        for the first time, or there are no more teeth and the line above simply walks off the
        chart it has been pinned to for {span}.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── what it cannot say ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">What it cannot say</p>
    <ul class="max-w-2xl space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li><b class="text-fg">It cannot tell you why.</b> The record holds how long each day was, not
        what made it so. Core, oceans, atmosphere, ice and tides all pull; separating them is the
        work of the papers cited above, not of this page.</li>
      <li><b class="text-fg">The recent end is provisional.</b> The last weeks of the series are
        rapid solutions and are revised as more observations land; a value near the right-hand edge
        can move slightly. The archive is dated and the figure is rebuilt from it, so a revision
        shows up as a redraw rather than as a silent correction.</li>
      <li><b class="text-fg">A millisecond is below the width of the line.</b> The thinning keeps
        the largest value in each bucket rather than the mean, so a one-day snap survives — but the
        plates are a shape, and the numbers beside them are the record.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <SheetFoot links={[
    { href: s.sources.eop, label: 'IERS EOP 14 C04, the series itself', external: true },
    { href: 'https://www.iers.org/IERS/EN/Publications/Bulletins/bulletins.html', label: 'IERS bulletins', external: true },
    { href: '/experiments', label: 'the lab' },
  ]}>
    <p class="max-w-2xl font-mono text-mono-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-faint">
      {s.days.toLocaleString('en-GB')} days, {s.first} to {s.last}, one row each, snapshotted
      {' '}{s.fetched} into <code>src/data/sawtooth/rotation.json</code> and committed — so every
      figure here is recomputable from this repository rather than from whether a server answers.
      Geometry: <code>src/lib/sawtooth/figure.ts</code>. No number on this page is typed.
    </p>
  </SheetFoot>
</main>
